teddyOS logs
============

Where to look when something goes wrong.

  /var/log/teddyos/app/           per-app rotating files (search, setup, …)
  /var/log/teddyos/snapshots/     frozen copies of journal + system state
  /var/log/teddyos/snapshots/latest  → most recent snapshot
  journalctl -b                   this boot
  journalctl -t 'teddyos-*'       only teddyOS apps
  journalctl -b -1                previous boot (the one people reboot away from)

Consent
-------

Snapshots and the daily collector require the setup grant

  “Help improve teddyOS”  (diagnostics.share)

Off by default. Without it, `teddyos-log-collect` exits without writing.
Operators on a machine they own can pass --force.

Take a fresh snapshot (if granted):

  sudo teddyos-log-collect

From the Mac (host), pull everything off a running guest:

  ./scripts/collect-os-logs.sh

Snapshots keep about 20 newest. Journal keeps ~14 days / 512 MB.
